1. Terraform Intro, State File, Variables & Outputs

Scenario: “What happens if two engineers run Terraform at the same time?”

“Terraform uses the state file to track infrastructure and determine what changes are required. In a team environment, I would store the state remotely, 
for example in an S3 backend, and enable state locking to prevent concurrent Terraform operations. This avoids state corruption and conflicting infrastructure changes.”

Scenario: “Why do you use variables?”

“I use variables to make Terraform configurations reusable across environments. For example, instead of hardcoding an EC2 instance type, I can define it as a 
variable and provide different values for Dev, QA, and Production.”

2. Aliases, Providers, Security Groups & Outputs

Scenario: “You need to deploy resources in two AWS regions. How would you do it?”

“I would configure multiple AWS provider blocks using aliases. For example, one provider could target Mumbai and another Singapore. Then I can explicitly specify 
which provider a resource should use. This allows the same Terraform configuration to manage resources across multiple regions.”

Security Group scenario:

“I would create security groups with only the required inbound and outbound rules. For example, an application server may allow HTTPS from the load balancer, while 
SSH access would be restricted to an approved administration network.”

3. Workspace, Local & Refresh

Scenario: “How would you manage Dev and QA using Terraform?”

“Terraform workspaces can maintain separate state files for different environments while using the same configuration. For example, I could have dev and qa workspaces
with different variable values. However, for larger production environments, I generally prefer separate configurations or repositories when stronger isolation is required.”

Local scenario:

“I use Terraform locals when I need to define reusable expressions or computed values within the configuration. This helps reduce repetition and makes the 
Terraform code cleaner.”

4. Import, Lifecycle & Dynamic Blocks

Scenario: “An AWS resource already exists but wasn't created using Terraform. Now you need Terraform to manage it. What would you do?”

“I would use terraform import to associate the existing resource with Terraform state. After importing, I would make sure the Terraform configuration accurately represents the existing infrastructure and run terraform plan to identify any differences before making changes.”

Lifecycle scenario:

“If I have a critical resource where accidental deletion is unacceptable, I can use prevent_destroy = true. I can also use create_before_destroy when I want Terraform 
to create the replacement resource before destroying the old one, where the resource supports that behavior.”

5. Provisioners

Scenario: “Would you use Terraform provisioners to install software on EC2?”

“I generally avoid provisioners because Terraform is primarily designed to provision infrastructure, not configure servers. I would prefer tools such as Ansible, cloud-init,
or AWS Systems Manager. Provisioners are more of a last resort when there isn't a better native solution.”

